# @@PROJECT@@

Standalone React + FastAPI development skeleton. The application has no harness
runtime dependency. Docker Compose, Python and npm are the normal toolchain.

## Start and stop

Requires Docker with Compose supporting `up --wait` and GNU Make.

```sh
make up
make down
```

Open [the web app](http://localhost:5173) and
[interactive API documentation](http://localhost:5173/api/docs).
The web page calls the API through Vite's `/api` proxy on the same origin.
The API is also reachable directly at [its health endpoint](http://localhost:8000/api/health).
Both published ports bind to 127.0.0.1, not all network interfaces.

`.env` is optional. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` to override `WEB_PORT`, `API_PORT`
or `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME`. Choose distinct ports and Compose names for concurrent
copies. No credentials are required. Never commit secrets to `project.yaml` or
the frontend; Vite's public environment variables are visible in browser code.

`make up` builds images, starts both services and waits for healthy containers.
It fails if startup checks do not pass. The first run downloads pinned dependencies
and official image digests. Subsequent builds use Docker's cache.
Code is copied into images: run `make up` again after changing source code.
`make down` removes this Compose project's containers/network, not images or data
from unrelated projects. No persistent volumes exist in this skeleton.

## Commands

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `make up` / `make down` | Start and stop the complete stack |
| `make frontend-up` / `make frontend-down` | Start web (and required API) / stop web |
| `make backend-up` / `make backend-down` | Start / stop API |
| `make logs` / `make status` | Follow logs / inspect containers |
| `make test` | API pytest tests and web Node tests, in disposable containers |
| `make lint` | Python Ruff; web TypeScript checking and Prettier |
| `make format` | Format host source through explicit bind mounts |
| `make build` | Build images and compile the frontend bundle |

There are no worker/scheduler commands because those processes are not enabled.
Tests and lint can run before `make up`: each target builds its required image.
Use `COMPOSE='docker compose -p other-project'` to override the Compose command.

## Without Docker

Use Python 3.12 and Node 24. Two terminals:

```sh
cd services/@@API@@
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements-dev.txt
.venv/bin/python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000
```

```sh
cd apps/@@WEB@@
npm ci
npm run dev
```

API tests: `.venv/bin/python -m pytest` in the API directory.
Frontend tests/build: `npm test` and `npm run build` in the web directory.
The Vite proxy defaults to `http://127.0.0.1:8000`; use `API_INTERNAL_URL` to
override its server-side target. This variable is not a browser API credential.

## Layout and scope

```text
apps/@@WEB@@/       React, TypeScript, Vite, web tests and Dockerfile
services/@@API@@/  FastAPI health endpoint, OpenAPI, tests and Dockerfile
compose.yaml       Development process wiring and health checks
Makefile           Stable local commands
project.yaml       Declarative intent, not a runtime dependency
.harness/          Template provenance and file hashes; not loaded by the app
```

The API includes a real health endpoint. There is no business logic,
database, authentication, admin, AI provider, worker, scheduler or cloud setup.
When `architecture.hexagonal_services` is true (the default), see
`services/@@API@@/ARCHITECTURE.md` for the readiness use case, injectable ports,
adapters and contract tests. Run `make test-contracts` for that variant.
When false, the service remains a simple FastAPI health endpoint.

The default web container runs Vite for local development; it is not a production
web server. `npm run build` creates static assets in `apps/@@WEB@@/dist` when run
locally. The API Dockerfile has a `runtime` target without test tools; Compose
uses `development` so tests and lint work out of the box. There is no deployment
workflow and no production-readiness claim.

Direct package versions and transitive dependencies are pinned in
`package-lock.json` and the hashed `requirements*.txt` files. Keep these locks
under version control. Their `.in` inputs record direct Python dependencies.
Changing dependencies requires deliberate lock regeneration and revalidation.
